-- Migration: Bloom's L3 rebalancing — FAR batch 1 (25 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-21
-- Purpose: Rewrite 25 L2 (Application) questions to L3 (Analysis) depth for FAR section
-- Affected topics: Revenue Recognition, Governmental Accounting, Consolidations, Equity, Income Taxes, Fixed Assets, Leases

BEGIN;

-- ============================================================
-- REVENUE RECOGNITION (4 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 35: Revenue Recognition — Bundled transaction price allocation with a discount
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Software sells a bundled package containing a software license and two years of technical support for $108,000. The standalone selling prices are $90,000 for the license and $30,000 for the support ($120,000 total). The sales manager argues that the $12,000 discount should be allocated entirely to the support obligation because the license price was never discounted in standalone sales. Under ASC 606, how should the $12,000 discount be allocated, and what effect does this have on the revenue recognized at the point the license is delivered?',
  choices = '["The discount is allocated entirely to the support obligation, so the license receives $90,000 at delivery — observable evidence that standalone license prices are never discounted satisfies the criteria for concentrating the discount in one obligation under ASC 606-10-32-37", "The discount must be allocated proportionally based on relative standalone selling prices — the license receives $81,000 ($90,000 / $120,000 x $108,000) and the support receives $27,000, reducing upfront revenue recognition by $9,000 compared to the sales manager''s approach", "The discount is allocated entirely to the license because it is the larger performance obligation, resulting in $78,000 at delivery and $30,000 for support", "The discount cannot be allocated and instead reduces the total transaction price to $108,000, with all revenue deferred until both obligations are satisfied"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-32-33, the default method allocates the transaction price to each performance obligation based on relative standalone selling prices. The license receives $90,000 / $120,000 x $108,000 = $81,000, and support receives $30,000 / $120,000 x $108,000 = $27,000. While ASC 606-10-32-36 through 32-37 permit concentrating a discount in one obligation, this requires observable evidence that the entity regularly sells that obligation at a discount and the discount relates specifically to that obligation. The sales manager''s claim alone does not satisfy these criteria. The practical effect is that proportional allocation reduces upfront license revenue by $9,000 compared to concentrating the discount in support. (A) is wrong because merely noting that the license was never discounted in standalone sales is insufficient — the entity must also demonstrate that the discount bundle relates specifically to the support obligation. (C) is wrong because the discount is never allocated based on obligation size. (D) is wrong because ASC 606 requires allocation to separate obligations, not deferral of the entire amount.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 35;

-- ID 36: Revenue Recognition — Cost overrun effect on over-time revenue recognition
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Construction enters into a $10 million contract to build a bridge, with estimated total costs of $8 million. At year-end, $3 million in costs have been incurred. The project engineer then reports that unexpected soil conditions will increase total estimated costs to $9.5 million. How does the cost overrun affect revenue recognized to date under the cost-to-cost method, and what is the financial statement impact of the revised estimate compared to the original estimate?',
  choices = '["Revenue recognized decreases from $3,750,000 to $3,157,895 — the cost overrun lowers the completion percentage from 37.5% to 31.6%, reducing both reported revenue and gross profit, which illustrates how input-method estimates under ASC 606 are sensitive to changes in total expected costs", "Revenue recognized increases because the higher total costs accelerate the completion percentage, resulting in $4,200,000 recognized", "Revenue recognized remains $3,750,000 because ASC 606 locks in the original estimate and does not permit revisions until the contract is complete", "Revenue recognized drops to $3,000,000 because the cost overrun eliminates the profit margin entirely, requiring the company to recognize revenue equal to costs incurred"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under the cost-to-cost input method (ASC 606-10-55-20), the completion percentage equals costs incurred divided by total estimated costs. Originally: $3M / $8M = 37.5%, yielding revenue of $3,750,000. After the revision: $3M / $9.5M = 31.58%, yielding revenue of $3,157,895. The $592,105 reduction illustrates a critical analytical point — when total expected costs rise, the same incurred costs represent a smaller fraction of completion, compressing both revenue and gross profit in the current period. This is a cumulative catch-up adjustment under ASC 606-10-32-14. (B) is wrong because higher total costs decrease the completion percentage, not increase it. (C) is wrong because ASC 606 requires estimates to be updated each reporting period with changes recognized as cumulative adjustments. (D) is wrong because the contract still has a positive margin ($10M revenue vs $9.5M cost), so revenue is not limited to costs incurred.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 36;

-- ID 151: Revenue Recognition — Right of return effect on revenue and asset recognition
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Electronics sells 1,000 tablets at $500 each with a 30-day right of return. Historical data shows a 6% return rate. The cost per tablet is $300. The controller recognizes revenue for all 1,000 units and records a contingent liability for expected returns. Under ASC 606, what is the correct effect of the return right on the financial statements, and why is the controller''s approach incorrect?',
  choices = '["The controller''s approach is correct — recognizing full revenue with a contingent liability properly reflects the uncertainty of returns under ASC 450", "The controller should defer all $500,000 in revenue until the 30-day return period expires because the transaction price is fully variable", "Revenue should be $470,000 (940 units not expected to be returned), with a $30,000 refund liability and a $18,000 right-of-return asset — the controller''s error is using a contingent liability model instead of the variable consideration framework, which reduces revenue rather than creating a separate liability for the gross amount", "Revenue should be $500,000 with a $30,000 contra-revenue account and no refund liability, because the return right is an implicit price concession"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-55-22 through 55-27, a right of return is accounted for using the variable consideration framework, not the contingent liability model. The entity recognizes revenue only for units not expected to be returned: 940 x $500 = $470,000. A refund liability of $30,000 (60 units x $500) is recorded for expected returns, and a right-of-return asset of $18,000 (60 units x $300 cost) is recorded for the expected inventory recovery. The controller''s approach of recognizing full revenue with a contingent liability is incorrect because ASC 606 constrains the transaction price to exclude amounts expected to be refunded — it is a revenue measurement issue, not a separate liability issue. (A) is wrong because ASC 450 contingent liabilities do not apply to sales returns under ASC 606. (B) is wrong because the return estimate is not fully variable — the entity can reasonably estimate the 6% rate, so only the uncertain portion is excluded from revenue. (D) is wrong because a refund liability is required, not merely a contra-revenue account.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 151;

-- ID 153: Revenue Recognition — Contract modification vs variable consideration analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Software has a three-year license and maintenance contract with Ridgemont Financial for $900,000. Six months in, Ridgemont requests additional customization features and an extended support term. Both parties agree to increase the contract price by $150,000. Separately, the original contract includes a $50,000 performance bonus if uptime exceeds 99.9%. The controller is evaluating whether each change qualifies as a contract modification. Under ASC 606, how do the $150,000 scope change and the $50,000 performance bonus differ in their accounting treatment?',
  choices = '["Both are contract modifications because they change the total consideration expected under the contract", "The $150,000 is variable consideration because it depends on Ridgemont''s request, while the $50,000 bonus is a contract modification because it was added after inception", "The $150,000 scope change is a contract modification requiring evaluation of whether it creates a separate contract, while the $50,000 bonus is variable consideration estimated under the constraint — the distinction matters because a modification changes enforceable rights through mutual approval, whereas variable consideration is a remeasurement of existing contract terms", "Neither qualifies as a contract modification — the $150,000 is an option exercise and the $50,000 is a contingent asset recognized only when realized"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-25-10, a contract modification is a change approved by the parties that creates new or changes existing enforceable rights and obligations — the $150,000 expansion of scope and price meets this definition. The entity must then evaluate whether the modification is a separate contract (ASC 606-10-25-12) or an adjustment to the existing contract. The $50,000 performance bonus, by contrast, was embedded in the original contract terms and represents variable consideration under ASC 606-10-32-5, estimated using either the expected value or most likely amount method and subject to the constraint. The analytical distinction is critical: modifications require reassessment of performance obligations and allocation, while variable consideration requires ongoing estimation within the existing contract framework. (A) is wrong because not all changes in expected consideration are modifications — variable consideration is inherent in the original terms. (B) reverses the classification. (D) is wrong because the scope change is not an option exercise — it required new mutual approval, which defines a modification.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 153;

