-- ID 177: Governmental Accounting — Current vs economic resources measurement focus comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Ridgemont purchases a $2.4 million fire truck using general fund appropriations and simultaneously issues $5 million in general obligation bonds for a new community center. The newly hired staff accountant records both the fire truck as a capital asset and the bonds as a long-term liability in the general fund statements. The supervisor explains these entries are incorrect. Under GASB 34, how does the current financial resources measurement focus in governmental funds affect the reporting of these two transactions, and where do the long-term items appear?',
  choices = '["The accountant is correct — governmental funds report both capital assets and long-term debt because the economic resources measurement focus applies to all government financial statements", "The general fund reports the fire truck purchase as an expenditure (not a capital asset) and the bond proceeds as an other financing source (not a long-term liability) — long-term assets and liabilities are excluded from governmental fund statements and reported only in the government-wide statements, which use the economic resources measurement focus", "The general fund reports the fire truck as a capital asset but excludes the bonds, because assets are always recognized in the fund that pays for them while debt is always reported at the government-wide level", "Both transactions are excluded from the general fund entirely and reported only in the capital projects fund, which uses the economic resources measurement focus"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus and modified accrual basis. This focus reports only current financial resources — cash and items expected to convert to cash soon. The fire truck purchase is reported as a capital outlay expenditure in the general fund (reducing fund balance), not as an asset. The bond proceeds are reported as an other financing source (increasing fund balance), not as a long-term liability. Both the capital asset and the long-term debt appear in the government-wide statement of net position, which uses the economic resources measurement focus and full accrual basis. The reconciliation between fund-level and government-wide statements explains these differences. (A) is wrong because the economic resources measurement focus does not apply to governmental funds. (C) is wrong because capital assets are never reported in governmental fund statements regardless of which fund pays. (D) is wrong because the general fund does report the cash transactions — it simply classifies them as expenditures and other financing sources rather than as assets and liabilities.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 177;

-- ID 178: Governmental Accounting — Availability criterion effect on property tax revenue recognition
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Thornfield levies $12 million in property taxes on January 1, 2026. By the December 31 fiscal year-end, $11.2 million has been collected. An additional $500,000 is expected in January (within 60 days) and $300,000 in March (beyond 60 days). The finance director records $12 million in property tax revenue in the general fund. Under GASB standards, how does the 60-day availability criterion affect the amount of revenue recognized in the general fund versus the government-wide statements, and what happens to the $300,000?',
  choices = '["Both the general fund and government-wide statements recognize $12 million because the full levy is measurable and the timing of collection is irrelevant under both bases of accounting", "The general fund recognizes $11.7 million and the government-wide statements recognize $12 million — the $300,000 collected beyond 60 days is reported as deferred inflows of resources in the general fund because it fails the availability test under modified accrual, while the government-wide statements recognize the full levy under full accrual since the enforceable claim exists at the levy date", "The general fund recognizes $11.2 million (cash collected only) and the government-wide statements recognize $11.7 million, because both bases impose availability requirements but with different windows", "Both statements recognize $11.7 million because GASB requires consistent revenue recognition across fund-level and government-wide reporting"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under modified accrual (GASB Statement No. 33), property tax revenue in governmental funds must be both measurable and available — collected within the period or soon enough thereafter (typically 60 days) to pay current liabilities. Thornfield recognizes $11.2M collected plus $500,000 available within 60 days = $11.7M as general fund revenue. The $300,000 expected in March is reported as deferred inflows of resources because it fails the availability criterion. In the government-wide statements (full accrual per GASB 34), the entire $12M levy is recognized as revenue when the enforceable legal claim arises, regardless of collection timing. The $300,000 difference between the two statements is a reconciling item explained in the conversion from fund-level to government-wide reporting. (A) is wrong because the availability criterion creates a real difference between the two reporting levels. (C) is wrong because modified accrual is not cash basis — the $500,000 within 60 days is recognized as revenue. (D) is wrong because GASB explicitly requires different measurement focuses at the fund and government-wide levels.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 178;

-- ID 180: Governmental Accounting — Bond proceeds fund classification and interfund analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Ashford issues $8 million in general obligation bonds to finance construction of a new fire station. The city council also authorizes a $500,000 transfer from the general fund to cover preliminary architectural costs before bond proceeds are available. The finance director must determine which funds are affected. How do the bond proceeds and the interfund transfer flow through the fund structure, and what is the net effect on each fund''s fund balance?',
  choices = '["The bond proceeds and the transfer both go to the general fund, increasing its fund balance by $8.5 million, because general obligation debt is serviced from general revenues", "The $8 million bond proceeds are reported as an other financing source in the capital projects fund, and the $500,000 is reported as a transfer out (reducing general fund balance) and a transfer in (increasing capital projects fund balance) — the capital projects fund balance increases by $8.5 million while the general fund balance decreases by $500,000", "The $8 million goes to the debt service fund because all bond-related transactions must be recorded in the fund responsible for debt repayment, and the $500,000 remains in the general fund as a construction expenditure", "The $8 million is split between the capital projects fund ($7.5 million) and the debt service fund ($500,000 for the first interest payment), with the general fund transfer offset against the bond proceeds"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under GASB standards, bond proceeds for capital construction are reported in a capital projects fund, which accounts for resources restricted for acquisition or construction of major capital facilities. The $8 million appears as an other financing source (not revenue) in the capital projects fund. The $500,000 interfund transfer is reported as a transfer out in the general fund (reducing fund balance by $500,000) and a transfer in to the capital projects fund (increasing fund balance). The debt service fund is not involved at this stage — it accounts for principal and interest repayment, not construction. The net effect: the capital projects fund balance increases by $8.5 million ($8M bond proceeds + $500K transfer in), and the general fund balance decreases by $500,000. (A) is wrong because bond proceeds for capital construction go to the capital projects fund, not the general fund. (C) is wrong because the debt service fund handles repayment, not receipt of bond proceeds. (D) is wrong because bond proceeds are not split — the full amount goes to the fund using the resources for their restricted purpose.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 180;

-- ID 181: Governmental Accounting — GASB 87 single-model lease vs ASC 842 dual-model comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A county government leases office space for 10 years at $120,000 per year. A private company leases identical office space in the same building under the same terms. The government applies GASB 87 and the private company applies ASC 842. How does the lease classification and balance sheet presentation differ between the two entities, and why did GASB adopt a different approach than FASB?',
  choices = '["Both entities classify this as a finance lease and report identical right-of-use assets and lease liabilities, because GASB 87 and ASC 842 use the same classification criteria for leases exceeding 12 months", "Under GASB 87, the county recognizes a lease liability and an intangible right-to-use asset with no classification distinction, while under ASC 842 the company must evaluate five criteria to determine whether this is a finance or operating lease — GASB adopted a single model because governmental lease transactions rarely involve the transfer-of-ownership economics that justify the FASB''s dual classification", "The county recognizes only an operating expense under GASB 87 because governmental entities are exempt from lease capitalization, while the company must capitalize the lease under ASC 842", "Both entities use a single-model approach — the difference is only in terminology, with GASB calling it a ''lease asset'' and FASB calling it a ''right-of-use asset''"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): GASB 87 uses a single-model approach where all leases with terms greater than 12 months (barring exceptions) are recognized by recording a lease liability and an intangible right-to-use lease asset. There is no finance/operating distinction. The lessee amortizes the asset and the liability separately. Under ASC 842, the private company must evaluate five criteria (ownership transfer, purchase option, major part of economic life, substantially all of fair value, specialized asset) to classify the lease as finance or operating. Both classifications require balance sheet recognition, but expense patterns differ — finance leases front-load expense while operating leases recognize a single straight-line lease cost. GASB adopted the single model because government leases typically involve using assets rather than acquiring them, making the dual classification less meaningful. (A) is wrong because GASB 87 does not use finance/operating classifications. (C) is wrong because GASB 87 explicitly requires capitalization for qualifying leases. (D) is wrong because the difference is substantive — ASC 842''s dual model produces different expense patterns depending on classification.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 181;

-- ============================================================
-- CONSOLIDATIONS (4 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 38: Consolidations — LIFO reserve conversion effect on comparative analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Manufacturing uses LIFO and reports year-end inventory of $500,000 with a LIFO reserve of $80,000. Its competitor, Cascade Industries, uses FIFO and reports inventory of $620,000. An analyst is comparing the two companies'' inventory turnover ratios. Ridgemont''s cost of goods sold is $2,000,000 and Cascade''s is $2,100,000. How does converting Ridgemont to a FIFO basis affect the inventory turnover comparison, and what does the LIFO reserve reveal about the impact of rising prices on Ridgemont''s reported results?',
  choices = '["Converting Ridgemont to FIFO changes inventory to $580,000, which lowers its turnover ratio from 4.0 to approximately 3.45 — this reveals that LIFO understates inventory and overstates turnover during rising prices, making Ridgemont appear more efficient than it is when compared to the FIFO-based competitor", "No conversion is necessary because LIFO and FIFO produce the same inventory turnover when input prices are rising, as both methods track the same physical flow", "Converting Ridgemont to FIFO increases inventory to $580,000 and also increases COGS to $2,080,000, resulting in a higher turnover ratio of 3.59 that shows Ridgemont is more efficient than Cascade", "The LIFO reserve should be subtracted from inventory to produce a replacement cost basis of $420,000, resulting in a turnover ratio of 4.76 that is the most analytically appropriate comparison"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): To convert LIFO to FIFO for comparability: FIFO inventory = LIFO inventory + LIFO reserve = $500,000 + $80,000 = $580,000. Ridgemont''s LIFO-based turnover is $2,000,000 / $500,000 = 4.0. On a FIFO basis, turnover becomes $2,000,000 / $580,000 = 3.45 (using unadjusted COGS for the simplified conversion). Cascade''s turnover is $2,100,000 / $620,000 = 3.39. The conversion narrows the apparent efficiency gap substantially. The $80,000 LIFO reserve represents the cumulative effect of rising prices — during inflation, LIFO charges higher recent costs to COGS (reducing income and inventory) while FIFO carries older, lower costs in COGS. Without conversion, the analyst would overstate Ridgemont''s relative efficiency. (B) is wrong because LIFO and FIFO produce materially different inventory values and turnover ratios in rising-price environments. (C) is wrong because the simplified conversion adjusts inventory, not COGS — a full conversion would also adjust COGS downward, further lowering turnover. (D) is wrong because the LIFO reserve is added to LIFO inventory to approximate FIFO, not subtracted.',
  correct_index = 0
WHERE id = 38;

-- ID 164: Consolidations — Short-term lease exemption effect on balance sheet comparability
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont LLC leases office equipment for 10 months with no purchase option and elects the ASC 842 short-term lease exemption. Its competitor, Thornfield Corp, leases identical equipment for 13 months. Both leases require monthly payments of $5,000. An analyst is comparing the two companies'' balance sheets and leverage ratios. How does Ridgemont''s election of the short-term exemption affect the comparability of their financial statements, and what analytical adjustment should the analyst consider?',
  choices = '["The analyst needs no adjustment because both companies report the lease payments as operating expenses, producing comparable income statements and balance sheets", "Ridgemont''s balance sheet excludes the right-of-use asset and lease liability that Thornfield must recognize — this makes Ridgemont appear less leveraged, and the analyst should consider adding back the estimated lease obligation to Ridgemont''s liabilities for a comparable leverage analysis, since the 12-month threshold creates an artificial distinction between economically similar arrangements", "Thornfield''s 13-month lease should also qualify for the short-term exemption because ASC 842 rounds lease terms to the nearest year, eliminating the comparability issue", "The comparability issue is immaterial because short-term leases are always insignificant relative to total assets and liabilities"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842-20-25-2, the short-term lease exemption applies only to leases with a term of 12 months or less at commencement. Ridgemont''s 10-month lease qualifies, so no right-of-use asset or lease liability is recognized — payments are expensed straight-line over the term. Thornfield''s 13-month lease exceeds the threshold and requires recognition of both a right-of-use asset and a lease liability on the balance sheet. This creates a comparability gap: Thornfield appears more leveraged despite having a nearly identical economic commitment. Analytically, the 12-month bright line is an arbitrary cutoff — a 10-month and 13-month lease for identical equipment at identical rates represent similar economic substance. The analyst should consider constructive capitalization of Ridgemont''s off-balance-sheet lease to produce comparable leverage metrics. (A) is wrong because Thornfield must recognize on-balance-sheet items while Ridgemont does not, creating fundamentally different balance sheet presentations. (C) is wrong because ASC 842 does not round lease terms — the 12-month threshold is a strict cutoff. (D) is wrong because the materiality of short-term leases depends on the entity''s circumstances and cannot be assumed immaterial.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 164;

-- ID 166: Consolidations — Sales-type lease effect on lessor income recognition pattern
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Evergreen Equipment Co. leases a machine with a carrying amount of $80,000 and a fair value of $110,000 under a sales-type lease. The lease term is 5 years with annual payments of $25,000, and the implicit rate produces a present value of lease payments equal to fair value. If the same machine were leased under an operating lease instead, how would the lessor''s income recognition pattern differ over the 5-year term, and what drives the timing difference?',
  choices = '["There is no difference — both classifications produce the same total income over the lease term, recognized in the same pattern each year", "Under the sales-type lease, Evergreen recognizes a $30,000 selling profit at commencement plus interest income over 5 years, while an operating lease would recognize $25,000 in straight-line rental income annually — the sales-type lease front-loads income recognition because the transfer of control justifies immediate profit recognition, whereas the operating lease spreads the return evenly because the lessor retains the asset", "Under the sales-type lease, Evergreen defers all profit until the lease expires and the asset is returned, while the operating lease recognizes profit immediately as each payment is received", "Under both classifications, Evergreen recognizes $25,000 per year in lease income, but the sales-type lease also requires recognizing a $30,000 impairment loss on the underlying asset at commencement"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842-30-25-1, a sales-type lease requires the lessor to derecognize the underlying asset, recognize a net investment in the lease, and record a selling profit at commencement. Evergreen recognizes a $30,000 profit ($110,000 fair value minus $80,000 carrying amount) at inception, plus interest income over 5 years as the net investment unwinds. Under an operating lease (ASC 842-30-25-11), the lessor retains the asset on its balance sheet, depreciates it, and recognizes $25,000 in rental income each year on a straight-line basis. Total income over 5 years is identical ($125,000 payments minus $80,000 cost = $45,000), but the timing differs dramatically: the sales-type lease front-loads a $30,000 profit at inception, while the operating lease spreads recognition evenly. The classification reflects whether control of the underlying asset has transferred to the lessee. (A) is wrong because while total income is the same, the recognition pattern differs significantly. (C) reverses the treatment — the sales-type lease accelerates profit, not defers it. (D) is wrong because the $30,000 is a selling profit, not an impairment loss.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 166;

-- ID 167: Consolidations — Intercompany elimination effect on consolidated net income
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Parent Corp owns 100% of Subsidiary Inc. During the year, Subsidiary sold inventory to Parent for $400,000 that cost Subsidiary $280,000. By year-end, Parent has resold 75% of this inventory to outside customers but still holds 25% in ending inventory. Parent reports standalone net income of $600,000 and Subsidiary reports $350,000. What effect do the intercompany elimination entries have on consolidated net income, and why is the elimination necessary?',
  choices = '["Consolidated net income is $950,000 ($600,000 + $350,000) with no elimination needed because the intercompany sale was at arm''s length pricing", "Consolidated net income is $920,000 — the $30,000 unrealized profit in ending inventory (25% of the $120,000 gross profit) must be eliminated because from a consolidated perspective the inventory has not yet been sold to an outside party, and recognizing profit on goods still within the consolidated group would overstate earnings", "Consolidated net income is $830,000 because the entire $120,000 intercompany gross profit must be eliminated regardless of how much inventory was resold to outsiders", "Consolidated net income is $950,000 minus the full $400,000 intercompany sale, resulting in $550,000, because all intercompany revenue must be removed from consolidation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): In consolidation, intercompany transactions are eliminated to present the group as a single economic entity. Subsidiary earned a gross profit of $120,000 ($400,000 - $280,000) on the intercompany sale. Since Parent resold 75% to outside customers, that portion of profit ($90,000) is realized from the consolidated perspective. However, the 25% still in Parent''s ending inventory contains $30,000 of unrealized intercompany profit ($120,000 x 25%). This $30,000 must be eliminated from consolidated net income and ending inventory must be reduced to Subsidiary''s original cost. Consolidated net income: $600,000 + $350,000 - $30,000 = $920,000. (A) is wrong because arm''s length pricing is irrelevant — the consolidated entity cannot earn profit by transacting with itself. (C) is wrong because only the unrealized portion (inventory not yet sold externally) is eliminated; the 75% resold to outsiders represents realized profit. (D) is wrong because intercompany revenue and COGS are both eliminated, netting to zero on the income statement — only the unrealized gross profit in ending inventory affects consolidated net income.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 167;

