-- ID 161: Leases — R&D expensing effect on profitability and asset comparability
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Pharmaceuticals spent $2.3 million on early-stage laboratory research in 2025, while Cascade Biotech spent $2.3 million on development activities for a drug that has received FDA Phase 3 approval. Both companies report $10 million in revenue. Under ASC 730, Thornfield expenses the entire amount. If Cascade were applying IFRS (IAS 38) rather than U.S. GAAP, it could capitalize its development costs. How does this accounting difference affect the comparability of the two companies'' profitability and total assets, and what analytical adjustment would an investor make?',
  choices = '["There is no comparability issue because both U.S. GAAP and IFRS require all R&D costs to be expensed as incurred regardless of the development stage", "Under U.S. GAAP both companies expense $2.3 million, producing comparable income statements — but under IFRS, Cascade could capitalize its Phase 3 development costs, which would increase its reported assets by $2.3 million and its net income by $2.3 million (pre-amortization) compared to GAAP, illustrating how the GAAP/IFRS R&D treatment creates a systematic comparability gap for development-stage companies", "Thornfield could also capitalize under U.S. GAAP because ASC 730 permits capitalization once a compound enters clinical trials, eliminating the cross-standard comparability issue", "The IFRS treatment produces lower profitability for Cascade because capitalized development costs are amortized faster than expensing, front-loading total charges"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 730-10-25, virtually all R&D costs are expensed as incurred under U.S. GAAP, regardless of development stage. Both Thornfield and Cascade would expense $2.3 million against 2025 revenue. However, under IAS 38, development costs (as distinct from research costs) can be capitalized when six criteria are met — including technical feasibility, which Cascade''s Phase 3 approval likely satisfies. If Cascade applied IFRS, it would capitalize the $2.3 million as an intangible asset rather than expensing it, boosting net income by $2.3 million (before future amortization) and increasing total assets. This GAAP/IFRS divergence means that cross-border pharmaceutical comparisons are distorted: IFRS companies in development stages report higher income and assets. An analyst should either capitalize U.S. GAAP R&D or expense IFRS development costs to achieve comparability. (A) is wrong because IFRS permits development cost capitalization when criteria are met. (C) is wrong because ASC 730 does not permit capitalization based on clinical trial stage. (D) is wrong because capitalization defers expense recognition, producing higher near-term profitability.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 161;

-- ID 162: Leases — Software capitalization threshold effect on income timing
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Games is developing a video game for external sale. Total project costs are $4 million: $1.5 million incurred before technological feasibility (completing the detailed program design) and $2.5 million after feasibility through general release. A competitor, Thornfield Studios, spent the same $4 million but established technological feasibility earlier, with only $800,000 pre-feasibility and $3.2 million post-feasibility. Under ASC 985-20, how does the timing of the technological feasibility milestone affect each company''s reported expenses and assets, and what does this reveal about the subjectivity of the standard?',
  choices = '["Both companies capitalize $4 million because ASC 985-20 requires capitalization of all software development costs regardless of the feasibility milestone", "Ridgemont capitalizes $2.5 million and Thornfield capitalizes $3.2 million — the earlier Thornfield establishes feasibility, the more costs shift from immediate expense to capitalized asset, increasing near-term profitability and total assets, which reveals that the feasibility determination is a significant judgment that directly affects the comparability of financial statements between companies developing similar products", "Both companies expense the full $4 million because ASC 985-20 only permits capitalization after the first sale, not after technological feasibility", "Ridgemont capitalizes $1.5 million (pre-feasibility) and expenses $2.5 million (post-feasibility) because costs before the milestone are more certain and therefore capitalizable"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 985-20, costs incurred before technological feasibility are expensed as R&D (ASC 730), and costs after technological feasibility through general release are capitalized. Ridgemont expenses $1.5M and capitalizes $2.5M. Thornfield expenses $800K and capitalizes $3.2M. Despite identical total spending, Thornfield reports $700,000 less in current-period expense and $700,000 more in capitalized assets. This asymmetry arises solely from when each company determines technological feasibility — a judgment call that can vary based on documentation practices, development methodology, and management discretion. The standard''s reliance on a subjective milestone means that two companies with identical projects and identical spending can report materially different profitability. Analysts should scrutinize the feasibility determination when comparing software companies. (A) is wrong because pre-feasibility costs must be expensed. (C) is wrong because capitalization begins at technological feasibility, not at first sale. (D) reverses the treatment — pre-feasibility costs are expensed, not capitalized.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 162;

-- ID 163: Leases — Finance vs operating lease effect on lessee financial ratios
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Logistics leases a fleet of trucks for 5 years at $200,000 per year. The present value of lease payments is $850,000. Under ASC 842, the company must determine whether this is a finance lease or an operating lease. Both classifications require recognizing a right-of-use asset and lease liability of $850,000 at commencement. How does the classification affect the income statement expense pattern and the debt-to-equity ratio over the lease term, and why do the two models produce different total expenses despite identical cash flows?',
  choices = '["Both classifications produce identical expense patterns and identical effects on the debt-to-equity ratio because ASC 842 requires the same balance sheet recognition regardless of classification", "A finance lease front-loads total expense because amortization is straight-line while interest is higher in early years (based on the declining liability balance), producing higher combined expense early and lower expense later — an operating lease recognizes a single straight-line lease cost each year, producing level expense — total expense over 5 years is identical ($1,000,000), but the timing affects reported profitability and leverage metrics, with the finance lease showing lower income in early years and a faster-declining liability", "A finance lease produces lower total expense over the lease term because the interest component is tax-deductible, reducing the after-tax cost below the operating lease total", "An operating lease produces higher total expense because the straight-line cost includes an implicit premium for the simpler accounting treatment under ASC 842"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 842, both classifications recognize an $850,000 right-of-use asset and lease liability at commencement. A finance lease records two separate expenses: straight-line amortization of the ROU asset and interest on the lease liability (front-loaded because interest is calculated on a declining balance). Combined expense is higher in early years and lower in later years. An operating lease recognizes a single straight-line lease cost ($200,000/year), allocated between interest on the liability and amortization of the ROU asset as a plug. Total expense over 5 years is the same ($1,000,000 = $200,000 x 5 years in cash payments). The finance lease''s front-loaded pattern depresses early-year income and shows a faster-declining liability on the balance sheet, which marginally improves the debt-to-equity ratio sooner. The classification choice affects the timing of expense recognition and the pattern of leverage reduction, not the total economic cost. (A) is wrong because the expense patterns differ meaningfully between classifications. (C) is wrong because the total pre-tax expense is identical under both classifications. (D) is wrong because no implicit premium exists — both produce the same total cost.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 163;

COMMIT;
